.TH GORGE 1
.SH NAME
gorge \- fly a canyon, in real 3-D
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B games/gorge
[
.B -t
|
.B -b
|
.B -s
] [
.B -q
] [
.B -S
|
.B -D
] [
.B -n
.I frames
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Gorge
is the other half of the argument the four games beside it make.
.IR Rivet (1),
.IR sprocket (1),
.IR starling (1)
and
.IR orchard (1)
are 8-bit in shape:
.IR nes (2)
composites tiles once at the destination's scale and every frame is
blits into an offscreen surface.
That is deliberate, and it is also what
.B doc/draw3d-adoption-plan.md
names as getting no GPU at all.
.PP
.I Gorge
has no tiles and no palette.
The canyon is geometry \- a floor and two walls, one quad per segment,
about two hundred faces a frame \- lit per vertex and handed to
.IR draw3d (2)'s
.I fillpoly3g
straight into the window image, which is the only destination
.IR emu (1)'s
Metal backend will take.
Colour is whatever the display's own channels can express.
The brightness ramp down the corridor is what makes a Gouraud fill worth
having: the same scene flat-shaded per face bands visibly at every
segment boundary.
.PP
The one thing here that is a sprite and not geometry is the explosion,
and it is a sprite on purpose:
.I sprite3zb
turns a flat image to face the eye and depth-tests it against everything
already drawn, so a burst behind a pylon is hidden by it.
The image is built at startup rather than opened, so this game carries
no asset file at all.
.PP
Fly with the arrow keys or
.BR wasd ,
fire with
.BR x ,
.B .
or the space bar.
Shooting a pylon scores 150 and flying past one scores 25; hitting one
costs a ship.
The canyon gets faster the further you get.
.B q
or Escape quits.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
emu\-cocoa \-r. /dis/games/gorge.dis
emu\-cocoa \-r. /dis/games/gorge.dis \-b
.EE
.PP
Run it as above and not under
.IR wm (1);
see BUGS.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/games/gorge.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR draw3d (2),
.IR draw3dbench (1),
.IR nes (2),
.IR rivet (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B -b
draws the same scene into the window as fast as it will go, with no
frame pacing, and reports what a frame costs.
On this host, 202 faces at 640 by 480:
.IP
.EX
/dis/math/draw3ddev.dis, no window manager   2408 us a frame
/dis/math/draw3d.dis                        73366 us a frame
.EE
.PP
That is a factor of thirty on the same geometry, and it is the whole
case for the provider:
the software one approximates a per-vertex fill by subdividing each
triangle four ways and flat-filling the leaves, while the device one
hands the face over as it stands and lets the hardware interpolate it.
.B -n
sets the number of frames timed;
.B -S
forces the software provider and
.B -D
the device one, for running the comparison.
.PP
.B -t
runs the self test, which needs no window.
It checks that the canyon never pinches shut, opens out or kinks between
segments and that the fog ramp spans its range;
that flying covers ground and moves the eye;
that steering moves the ship and all four walls stop it;
that one press makes one bolt and holding fire does not stream them;
that a bolt destroys a pylon, scores and leaves an explosion;
that flying into one costs a ship;
that pylons are recycled ahead rather than lost;
that the flight speeds up;
and that a frame draws something.
It steps every frame and draws one in ten \- drawing is a crash check
here, and the software provider is slow and allocates heavily enough
that a full run does not finish.
.PP
.B -s
plays a scripted opening headless and writes the frame to
.BR gorge.raw ,
.I w
by
.I h
of the display's own pixels, the size of one in the last line it prints.
.SH BUGS
.B The GPU is only reached with no window manager, and driving it under
.B one panics emu.
.IR Emu 's
Metal backend takes the screen image or a
.I fully clear
layer of it and nothing else, and a window manager's window is neither,
so under
.IR wm (1)
every face falls back to the CPU:
.B INFERNO_METAL_STATS=1
shows
.B g3_calls=0
for the whole run, and the device provider is then
.I slower
than the software one because it pays the wire cost on top.
Worse, driving the device provider under
.IR wm (1)
panics
.I emu
outright with
.BR "pushrun addrun" ,
reproducibly.
.PP
So this program chooses its provider from whether a window manager is
present, which it can tell because a window manager is the only thing
that hands its clients a
.BR Draw->Context .
Under
.IR wm (1)
it uses the software provider and says
.B software
on screen; started directly it uses the device one and says
.BR gpu .
.B -D
overrides that, and panics.
.PP
The panic has not been reduced below this program.
A loop of two hundred frames of a hundred and thirty Gouraud quads plus
a
.I sprite3zb
into a memory image, through the same provider, survives.
.PP
.B -s
and
.B -t
render through the software provider, so nothing automated here has ever
looked at what the GPU path actually puts on the screen \- and it cannot:
a
.I readpixels
after a GPU pass returns black, which is Phase 3 of
.BR doc/draw3d-adoption-plan.md .
.B g3_calls
says the geometry reached the hardware, not that it rasterized correctly;
.IR draw3ddemo (1)
records exactly that failure mode for
.IR frustum .
Those are two separate claims and only the first is evidenced here.
.PP
The canyon is generated from two sines, so it never repeats, and it is
also never interesting in a way a designer chose.
